<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The document worker is a PyInstaller onefile binary. It unpacks libpython and
  the rest of its dependencies into a temporary directory at startup and dlopens
  them from there. Those copies keep the signature PyInstaller gave them, not
  this team's, so under the hardened runtime that notarization requires, library
  validation refuses to map them:

    libpython3.12.dylib ... mapping process and mapped file (non-platform)
    have different Team IDs

  The worker then exits before reading a request, and the app reports only that
  it cannot load the list of supported formats.

  This is the narrowest exemption that fixes it. It applies to the whole bundle,
  which is a real cost: the main binary also stops checking who signed what it
  loads. Removing the need for it means dropping onefile packaging, so that the
  libraries live inside the bundle and are signed with it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
